[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Hotspots and the 500/50/5 Rule in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building scalable applications with Firebase Firestore, performance bottlenecks often arise from improper document write patterns. This tutorial explores the concept of 'hotspots' and how the famous 500/50/5 rule helps maintain optimal database performance."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Firestore Hotspot?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A hotspot occurs when a high volume of reads, writes, or deletes are directed at a very small subset of documents or a single collection within a short time frame. Firestore automatically splits and distributes data across multiple storage nodes based on document IDs. However, if your application continuously hammers sequential IDs or a single counter document, Firestore cannot scale horizontally fast enough, leading to contention, latency spikes, and transaction failures."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"The 500/50/5 Rule Explained"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 500/50/5 rule is a practical guideline provided by the Firebase engineering team to help developers avoid write hotspots. It defines safe operational thresholds for writing to a specific collection or document group over time."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"500 operations per second: The absolute maximum sustained write rate for a newly created collection before it has had time to automatically split."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"50 operations per second: A safer target for rapid ramp-ups on existing collections to prevent sudden bottlenecks."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"5 operations per second: The recommended limit for incremental updates to a single document or tightly grouped sequential IDs (like auto-incrementing counters)."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Anti-Pattern: The Single Counter Document"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"A common mistake is storing a global counter inside a single document to track metrics like total likes or active users. Every time an action occurs, the application updates this exact document, easily violating the 5 writes per second rule."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// BAD PRACTICE: Updating a single counter document constantly
const counterRef = db.collection('metrics').doc('global_likes');
await db.runTransaction(async (transaction) => {
  const doc = await transaction.get(counterRef);
  const newLikes = doc.data().likes + 1;
  transaction.update(counterRef, { likes: newLikes });
});"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Solution: Sharded Counters"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To respect the 500/50/5 rule when dealing with high-frequency writes, implement distributed or sharded counters. By spreading the write load across multiple subdocuments, you bypass the bottleneck of a single document."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"// GOOD PRACTICE: Incrementing a random shard
function incrementShard(docId) {
  const shardId = Math.floor(Math.random() * 10).toString();
  const shardRef = db.collection('metrics').doc('global_likes').collection('shards').doc(shardId);

  return shardRef.set({
    count: firebase.firestore.FieldValue.increment(1)
  }, { merge: true });
}"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By understanding hotspots and strictly adhering to the 500/50/5 rule through patterns like sharding, you ensure your Firebase Firestore architecture remains resilient, performant, and capable of scaling to millions of users without throttling errors."}]
